+Every `button` element takes the calibrated button surface and its states from the `elements`
+layer, whatever class it carries: an empty `class` attribute, a utility class, a class of your own,
+and a `data-bs-target` attribute each leave the surface in place. The layer also writes the
+release's reboot on every button: no margin, no text transform, the button appearance, the pointer
+cursor while enabled, and no outline on a focus the browser does not mark as visible. Each class
+the release builds on a `button` element writes the button reboot back on its button form. The
+`button-reboot` mixin writes, for every property the surface writes at rest or in a state, the
+release's value: the parent's font family, size, and line height, square corners, and the
+browser's own value for the rest. Each partial includes it on a `:where()` selector, which has
+zero specificity, in the `components` layer. That layer outranks the `elements` layer whatever the
+selector, so the reboot outranks every surface state, and every rule the class writes outranks the
+reboot. So a disabled `button.nav-link` element reads the same opacity and type as the disabled
+`a.nav-link` element beside it. The classes are the `btn-close`, `navbar-toggler`,
+`accordion-button`, `dropdown-item`, `nav-link`, `list-group-item`, `page-link`,
+`carousel-control-prev`, and `carousel-control-next` classes. A resting carousel indicator carries
+no class, so its reboot sits on the release's own `.carousel-indicators [data-bs-target]`
+selector, and a `data-bs-target` button outside the indicators keeps the surface. The `btn` class
+writes every property the surface writes, at rest and in each state, so it takes no reboot. The
+surface declares nothing important, so a class of your own in a later layer, or in none,
+overrides the surface property it names without a specificity contest. See
+[the button proof](../tests/src/styles/elements/button.test.ts).
 
